#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105592 :question/id #uuid "23793048-621c-5ab3-97ac-7e635553c947" :question/math-academy-id "e-4346" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ if $A(-1,1,0),$ $B(1,1,2),$ $C(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude (height) can be related to the area of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding altitude of the triangle.\n\nFor the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ we have $h=CH,$ and $b=AB.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =4i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula $A_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9331987e-8676-5bdb-b4ce-2129b1b09a8c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049388 :topic/math-academy-id 1397 :topic/title "Areas of Triangles"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b1cac9a-d8ad-501d-b0a6-a0a1bebe9636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285345} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf88e45-9bac-51c6-a90f-f80f508a8095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285363} {:answer/id #uuid "46574c64-b2d9-52a5-ba20-5c4e8f687459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285432} {:answer/id #uuid "61a98581-4f83-59f8-a0f9-f6ec11524a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285684} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd903e1-1e62-5f4c-86df-6c375dc3361d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b1cac9a-d8ad-501d-b0a6-a0a1bebe9636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285345} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b8bbd2-699b-5127-b6f0-02f2e8c72c29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287551}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95f6f3a1-caa9-5902-b6fb-cb14398f3fb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20027" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,1,2)$, $B(1,2,1)$, $C(3,2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i+2j+k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(3i+2j+3k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))j+((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =2i-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91d02db9-e77b-5b70-bf52-95157c6b371c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{30}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102627} {:answer/id #uuid "b8541fc0-8462-5904-9145-c20fc56387a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102628} {:answer/id #uuid "c06d702c-89e5-5852-9582-6fca6d927ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102629} {:answer/id #uuid "36fa653c-a96d-5d43-9c00-e68c6671ede2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102630} {:answer/id #uuid "376be9ee-03ee-58c2-9786-34782438fca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102631}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dc647480-b80a-5e28-afb7-8bed712a7f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102626}] :question/id #uuid "74c42862-54b5-59d3-9fc0-d15a552d30c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-20028" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $RH$ of the $△PRQ$, if $P(1,-2, 3)$, $Q(3, 2, 3)$, $R(2, 1, 4)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033166a8-eb6e-5026-9379-74758cf8f63e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{33}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259903} {:answer/id #uuid "46f34cae-0d7f-57af-bd21-c29c4d58eb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260491} {:answer/id #uuid "86df7a60-c683-5984-ab1c-7b2102dfbe16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261051} {:answer/id #uuid "8941d219-3334-51f2-895b-7a537acbf651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261070} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fd4792-88e8-5bab-8423-291a5ffe487b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9fd4792-88e8-5bab-8423-291a5ffe487b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261661} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af9ae3c-d3ff-5c29-96c7-03340f576f82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262143}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3898211-8ec0-5bec-b86d-5fc41284c16c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128006" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(4,-1,1),$ $B(0,-3,4),$ $C(2,1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3j+4k)-(4i-j+k) \\\\  & =-4i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+j+4k)-(4i-j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)i-((-4)\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 3)j+((-4)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-12i+6j-12k \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{18}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ 18 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17263848-7dad-541b-ad58-74745572f296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{38}}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260076} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfb5bcd-7bf0-53b0-a60b-2f17c4f73037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{38}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260133} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0f2dce-a930-5b09-bde7-49e546203c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260526} {:answer/id #uuid "803e61e6-6963-5b24-9fb8-5a3e08371ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260987} {:answer/id #uuid "8d500b7a-085a-5399-943c-18364bfccf23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "803e61e6-6963-5b24-9fb8-5a3e08371ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260987} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8984f2f-6da2-54bf-9096-9f18e7aa0c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262479}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbbc0374-2f40-56ea-b57e-f5227b194e10" :question/math-academy-id "q-128008" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $RH$ of the $△\\,PRQ,$ if $P(3,0,4),$ $Q(2,3,1),$ $R(0,3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $RH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,PQR$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $RH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(3i+4k) \\\\  & =-i+3j-3j \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(3j+k)-(3i+4k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+((-1)\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =6j+6k \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $PQ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{2} & =RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29485b5b-c665-5779-a34e-0364eb6fa1d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260238} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd34242-8e9e-5678-97c9-7cc01e6d4b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260525} {:answer/id #uuid "80b164c6-bba8-504a-bd8b-ab45cf5b2c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260996} {:answer/id #uuid "83f99092-f762-5530-8c3c-6fee86481012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261022} {:answer/id #uuid "a02e3ad1-637a-5291-aabd-24871e6db04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a02e3ad1-637a-5291-aabd-24871e6db04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261265} :answer-field/id #uuid "658ccd8b-267b-53ca-9ec1-baa7bb2e0bbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262297}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "acfa5ee8-797a-57cb-8b1c-947581dc04d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-128013" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of the $△\\,LMN,$ if $L(-1,4,1),$ $M(0,3,4),$ $N(1,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(3j+4k)-(-i+4j+k) \\\\  & =i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(i+4j+2k)-(-i+4j+k) \\\\  & =2i+k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 3)j+(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-i+5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{30} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ MH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42aac3c5-ad83-55c9-bc67-806f2c57a155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42aac3c5-ad83-55c9-bc67-806f2c57a155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290908} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee54665e-3213-5a19-9284-f386f859abe3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292475}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1a70e2a-5e7c-521f-a8eb-605197f729a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-275225" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-2,0,-1)$, $B(0,-2,-1)$, $C(-3,-2,0).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2j-k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-3i-2j)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =-i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-2i-2j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{2}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c05366e-122f-5432-9545-0905387ebf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c05366e-122f-5432-9545-0905387ebf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295700} :answer-field/id #uuid "8653ad62-f824-5e0d-9daf-40a42762bc5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296909}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc3dd100-4787-576c-a303-83ee06838d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-316392" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $NP$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(3,-1,0)$, $M(0,2,1)$, $N(-2,1,-1).$\n\n$NP=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $NP$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $NP,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(2j+k)-(3i-j) \\\\  & =-3i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(-2i+j-k)-(3i-j) \\\\  & =-5i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-5,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2)i-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-5))j+((-3)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-5))k \\\\  & =-5i-8j+9k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-8,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{170}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ \\sqrt{170} & =NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ NP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285859fd-02f8-5227-baf4-1ae234928772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4186}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "285859fd-02f8-5227-baf4-1ae234928772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4186}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295490} :answer-field/id #uuid "96ea95b7-7464-5f8e-b5f7-4c89f5a83900" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296951}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "118c9278-fa2c-5ecd-aadf-b52c033ba66c" :question/math-academy-id "q-316395" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $UT$ of the $△\\,UVW$, if $U(-2,2,0)$, $V(1,-1,2)$, $W(3,0,-1).$\n\n$UT=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $UT$ can be related to the area of $△\\,UVW$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $UT,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW} & =w-v \\\\  & =(3i-k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+j-3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =u-v \\\\  & =(-2i+2j)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+(2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =7i+13j+9k \\\\  & =⟨7,13,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{13}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{299}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,UVW$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $VW$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}VW & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{299} & =UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ UT & =\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4186}}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1348e485-5031-58d0-9132-dc7a8e060aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1348e485-5031-58d0-9132-dc7a8e060aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295371} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a7753b-1420-53a5-8abd-6a0b570e42f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "041de7f5-8dc3-5943-9410-cd670ed64e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-316400" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $VL$ of the $△\\,TUV$, if $T(1,0,1)$, $U(3,2,-1)$, $V(-1,1,0).$\n\n$VL=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $VL$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VL,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =u-t \\\\  & =(3i+2j-k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =v-t \\\\  & =(-i+j)-(i+k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =0i+6j+6k \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =VL\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ VL & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ VL & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff61a221-719e-515d-a51f-fa366df765f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff61a221-719e-515d-a51f-fa366df765f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296631} :answer-field/id #uuid "49802bb4-f9df-54ac-8e86-92d7c96102d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296770}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e0cc38a-3052-5bd2-aef9-6d95ea796618" :question/math-academy-id "q-316409" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $IP$ of the $△\\,HIJ$, if $H(-1,2,0)$, $I(1,1,3)$, $J(0,-2,1).$\n\n$IP=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $IP$ can be related to the area of $△\\,HIJ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $IP,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{HI}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HJ} & =j-h \\\\  & =(-2j+k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =i-4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{HI} & =i-h \\\\  & =(i+j+3k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =2i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HJ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{HI} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1))i-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-11i-j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-11,-1,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{HI}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{HJ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{HI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-11)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{171} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,HIJ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{19}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $HJ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}HJ & =|\\overset{\\to }{HJ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{19}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,IP\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\sqrt{2}\\,IP \\\\ IP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a47b6ed-480c-5e70-aefa-cd3ffcab87ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{806}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a47b6ed-480c-5e70-aefa-cd3ffcab87ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{806}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295507} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd373048-f3c1-5400-8d49-9bc5ec200162" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3aadd472-6c53-5587-bcdd-18195d95d688" :question/math-academy-id "q-316553" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CK$ of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(1,0,1),$ $B(4,2,1),$ $C(2,3,2).$\n\n$CK=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+2j+k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+0k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+2k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)i-(3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)j+(3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =2i-3j+7k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{806}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7fbf2db-fc7b-5658-bc9b-3b51bd33b591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{110}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7fbf2db-fc7b-5658-bc9b-3b51bd33b591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{110}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296318} :answer-field/id #uuid "659b57b6-4355-5ee2-9530-0067f725247f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296845}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9fd89971-85c9-5581-b57b-a897961ff335" :question/math-academy-id "q-316556" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $HK$ of the $△\\,GHI,$ if $G(1,1,1),$ $H(3,2,4),$ $I(2,4,2).$\n\n$HK=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $HK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GHI$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI\n$$\n\nSo, to find $HK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI} & =i-g \\\\  & =(2i+4j+2k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =h-g \\\\  & =(3i+2j+4k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =8i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨8,-1,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GHI$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $GI$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}GI & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{110}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8a61533-070f-501b-8382-171a4281490f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{22}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8a61533-070f-501b-8382-171a4281490f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{22}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296320} :answer-field/id #uuid "51a19f72-85aa-5439-92e2-7ba79108e904" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296795}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "05e91010-8b10-50cc-b3a2-21f43d310a7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-316558" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $TK$ of the $△\\,RST,$ if $R(4,5,6),$ $S(7,4,5),$ $T(6,8,4).$\n\n$TK=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $TK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,RST$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS\n$$\n\nSo, to find $TK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =s-r \\\\  & =(7i+4j+5k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =3i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =t-r \\\\  & =(6i+8j+4k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)i-(3(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =5i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨5,4,11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{162} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,RST$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $RS$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{22}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bce9a27-f4cc-58b8-899b-0adcf77a3029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336444} {:answer/id #uuid "3154ea6f-18ff-5b87-b29e-4d415f805d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336503} {:answer/id #uuid "8127319e-f64a-5b03-9442-11dd8802bec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337204} {:answer/id #uuid "9c703731-367d-5c95-8dba-8382da5e1070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337446} {:answer/id #uuid "9ccd42e4-4e86-5ccb-92e1-e81125b47e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c703731-367d-5c95-8dba-8382da5e1070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337446} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6a4426-4d5c-5b55-8aaf-f5663e17d82d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338434}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2109dfb7-8989-5063-a0ad-becd345c3aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-71113" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(3,3,2)$, $M(1,1,2)$, $N(2,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(3i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(2i+2j+k)-(3i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072d3204-08b4-5aac-aca7-bdc773125774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{38}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336085} {:answer/id #uuid "62d5a2fd-d0ed-50a1-b9e1-530565c72f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336920} {:answer/id #uuid "8101cc36-fb71-50d7-ac71-47da39f98581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337201} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d8bec1-6d0d-54d7-ae45-5d08e1b8540e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337627} {:answer/id #uuid "e626fb9e-e952-533a-955b-4d34b400fc65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072d3204-08b4-5aac-aca7-bdc773125774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{38}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336085} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2232b9a-6e70-56f9-95d7-f89ee74428fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338675}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86adad36-11f0-5e43-80fd-01317a16e7e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71115" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $VH$ of the $△\\,TUV$, if $T(3,2,3)$, $U(0,1,0)$, $V(2,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $VH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =u-t \\\\  & =j-(3i+2j+3k) \\\\  & =-3i-j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =v-t \\\\  & =(2i+j+2k)-(3i+2j+3k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))i-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))j+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093467b9-0067-542c-b65c-633c1cfc4b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336113} {:answer/id #uuid "3859e15a-56e5-569e-825a-f822ed3eac71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336557} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2eb1b1-c7b8-5381-b418-d9244be8d489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336991} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d294a1-32a0-5b4f-94b7-ee30ffc9011a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337918} {:answer/id #uuid "de85db2c-4065-5f60-92b7-7fdad6d31158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d294a1-32a0-5b4f-94b7-ee30ffc9011a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337918} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d1b4065-470d-599f-81c3-c9f0fd87b16f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338536}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9999542b-9f9f-5d86-95f6-bcd8d4190efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-71116" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $ZH$ of the $△\\,WYZ$, if $W(2,3,2)$, $Y(1,2,1)$, $Z(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $ZH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,WYZ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY\n$$\n\nSo, to find $ZH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY} & =y-w \\\\  & =(i+2j+k)-(2i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =z-w \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(2i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-4j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))j+((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-3i+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WYZ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $WY$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WY & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ ZH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ZH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d340e7-ddb4-51cf-bbf6-01458c17f611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336084} {:answer/id #uuid "0d900ac9-ddc4-5d0b-8b81-7b341697e575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336159} {:answer/id #uuid "56354dd4-647e-56d7-a9fd-a95bbb4ca8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336825} {:answer/id #uuid "8125d199-48f3-5aad-b0c5-ab92c3d59078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337203} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8b3bae-3fde-5b2f-8f26-3e3448abad77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8125d199-48f3-5aad-b0c5-ab92c3d59078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337203} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6e1490-16c3-533e-862d-6938fcc3b920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338333}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f34d7ce2-3b18-5fda-a701-f95afc0575b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71117" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $GH$ of the $△\\,GSM$, if $S(1,-1,2)$, $G(-2,0,2)$, $M(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $GH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GSM$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM\n$$\n\nSo, to find $GH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG} & =g-s \\\\  & =(-2i+2k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+j \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =m-s \\\\  & =(i+j)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)i-((-3)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)j+((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-2i-6j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{76} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GSM$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{19}}{2}=\\sqrt{19}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $SM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}SM & =|\\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =GH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f3c7d45-2508-558a-a671-704a2f709aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336627} {:answer/id #uuid "733fefab-043d-5fe4-93ed-cf5c6a3298cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337070} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0454d2-6cd6-50e2-9a7f-17ac15f3a137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337812} {:answer/id #uuid "d3fb8394-0a4f-5554-bef2-b4565678aeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337886} {:answer/id #uuid "f7adc41f-f450-52b1-9898-009b7b23875a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3fb8394-0a4f-5554-bef2-b4565678aeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337886} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e3142c-b7af-51c6-a353-db8d28d28403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338622}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6da0a984-327a-597f-9055-73fa298d90dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71128" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $DH$ of the $△\\,DMA$, if $D(-1,2,1)$, $M(-1,0,-1)$, $A(1,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $DH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,DMA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA\n$$\n\nSo, to find $DH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD} & =d-m \\\\  & =(-i+2j+k)-(-i-k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =a-m \\\\  & =(i-2j+k)-(-i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 2)i-(0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2)j+(0\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =8i+4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{96} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DMA$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{2}=2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $MA$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MA & =|\\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =DH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ DH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ DH & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105593 :question/id #uuid "2f380683-0d93-541d-a07d-95a1d889c761" :question/math-academy-id "e-4347" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-3,2 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -1,0,5 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 1,2,3 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 2)i-(-1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1)j+(-1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-10i+8j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-10,8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨2,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-10)+(-3)\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ba6b6b5-ac1d-5a3a-876a-af51fdf50e0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954 :topic/math-academy-id 177 :topic/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Components"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132 :topic/math-academy-id 245 :topic/title "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7cc806-5d8c-52f5-8e9d-28150ae6a1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285339} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0d409a-ddf1-525b-a0d4-aa58e215fa46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285343} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3e507c-3caf-5e62-ac65-0cc55ade3ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286515} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d377fc-8087-5c10-96ba-7988c2d59520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286551} {:answer/id #uuid "da0f18b2-ddfb-59a1-8003-09695e06caea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7cc806-5d8c-52f5-8e9d-28150ae6a1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285339} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb44303e-1350-575a-9022-58d744cd9e96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287657}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54e446ca-f780-515b-91f0-06748b3e67bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-20063" :question/problem "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨-2,1,8⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,1,8⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6351a